RUNBOOK - etcd-restore  -  printed procedures with your cluster's names filled in - kdash never runs them
runbooks:  [1 etcd-restore]   2 cert-rotate    3 node-notready    4 api-unreachable    5 oidc-login    [ ] previous / next   (kdash --runbook NAME)

WHEN TO USE  -  etcd data is lost or corrupted, or the cluster must go back to a known state
  ! A restore rolls the WHOLE cluster back to the snapshot time: everything created or changed after it is lost.
  ! Restoring onto NEW hosts needs the ORIGINAL server token (it decrypts the bootstrap data in the snapshot).
  ! Snapshots contain the cluster CA keys and all Secrets: copy and store them like secrets.

SNAPSHOTS
  unknown (unavailable) - list them on a server: sudo rke2 etcd-snapshot ls

A. RESTORE ONTO THE EXISTING SERVERS  -  servers: dev

  STEP 0  prepare (nothing changes yet)
      read   [on dev] sudo rke2 etcd-snapshot ls  # confirm the snapshot is on this node
      read   [on dev] sudo cat /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/token  # save the server token in your secret store first - needed for any restore onto new hosts
      read   kdash --report  # evidence of the current state before you change it

  STEP 1  stop rke2-server on every server
      CHANGE [on dev] sudo systemctl stop rke2-server  # all servers must be down before the reset

  STEP 2  reset the cluster from the snapshot (on dev only)
      CHANGE [on dev] sudo rke2 server --cluster-reset --cluster-reset-restore-path=/var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/snapshots/<SNAPSHOT-NAME>  # wait for: Managed etcd cluster membership has been reset, restart without --cluster-reset flag now
      note   if config.yaml has etcd-s3 settings and you restore this LOCAL file, add --etcd-s3=false

  STEP 3  start rke2-server on dev
      CHANGE [on dev] sudo systemctl start rke2-server  # the reset flag file is removed on this normal start
      read   kubectl --context kdash-local get nodes -o wide  # wait until dev is Ready

  STEP 4  on every OTHER server: back up and delete the old etcd data
      CHANGE [on the other servers (none found)] sudo mv /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)  # RKE2 asks to back up and delete server/db on each peer (the docs show: sudo rm -rf /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/)

  STEP 5  start rke2-server on the other servers, one at a time
      CHANGE [on the other servers (none found)] sudo systemctl start rke2-server  # they rejoin the restored cluster; wait for Ready before the next

  STEP 6  verify
      read   kubectl --context kdash-local get nodes -o wide  # all servers and agents Ready
      read   kubectl --context kdash-local get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running,status.phase!=Succeeded  # what has not come back yet
      read   kdash --doctor && kdash --certs  # API, access and certificates
      note   nodes are restored from the snapshot too: delete nodes that no longer exist (kubectl --context kdash-local delete node NAME) and re-run workloads changed after the snapshot from Git / Flux

B. RESTORE ONTO NEW HOSTS  -  the old servers are gone
  ! You need the ORIGINAL server token: /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/token from an old server, or your secret store. Without it the restore fails; a different token in config.yaml stops RKE2 from starting.

  STEP 1  copy the snapshot to the new first server
      CHANGE [on NEW-S1] sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/snapshots && sudo cp <SNAPSHOT-FILE> /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/snapshots/  # for S3 snapshots skip this and pass the S3 flags

  STEP 2  reset from the snapshot with the original token
      CHANGE [on NEW-S1] sudo rke2 server --cluster-reset --cluster-reset-restore-path=/var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/snapshots/<SNAPSHOT-NAME> --token=<ORIGINAL-SERVER-TOKEN>  # the token can also be set in /etc/rancher/rke2/config.yaml

  STEP 3  start it, then join the other new servers with the same token
      CHANGE [on NEW-S1] sudo systemctl start rke2-server
      note   other servers: config.yaml with  server: https://NEW-S1:9345  and  token: <ORIGINAL-SERVER-TOKEN>, then systemctl start rke2-server
      CHANGE kubectl --context kdash-local delete node OLD-NODE-NAME  # old nodes come back from the snapshot as NotReady: delete those that no longer exist

NOTES  -  from the RKE2 documentation
  --cluster-reset WITHOUT --cluster-reset-restore-path only resets etcd membership to one member; no data is restored.
  A reset writes /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/reset-flag (blocks a second reset) and moves the old data to /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/etcd-old-<timestamp>.
  A newer RKE2 minor version can restore an older snapshot. Snapshots cover embedded etcd only.
  Default snapshot directory: /var/lib/rancher/rke2/server/db/snapshots
